<style|<tuple|beamer|fira-font|british|triangle-list|cqft-slides|preview-ref>>

<\body>
  <screens|<\hidden>
    <cqft-title-slide|Observables and field equations|Joint work with
    Sarah-Jean Meyer, with the use of AI|SISSA lectures, Lecture<nbsp>5 \V
    notes: Lecture<nbsp>5, Ÿ9, Appendices A\UB>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Where we are>

    \;

    <with|font-series|bold|Lecture<nbsp>4.> The interacting field at
    resolution <math|t> solves the FBSDE

    <\equation*>
      \<mathd\>\<Phi\><rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>\<mathd\>t+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t>=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>,
    </equation*>

    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>\<sim\>\<nu\><rsub|t>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>>.
    Filtering: <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>=J<rsub|t>\<Phi\><rsub|T>+\<zeta\><rsub|t>>,
    <math|J<rsub|t>=K*Q<rsub|t>>, <math|K=Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>>; forecast
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>\<Phi\><rsub|T>=\<Phi\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>F<rsub|t>>;
    regulator <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>-K>,
    <math|S<rsub|t>=Q<rsub|T>-Q<rsub|t>>; effective average action
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>; <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<phi\>><rsub|t>> the law of
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> given <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>=\<phi\>>, and
    <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>> its covariance.

    <with|font-series|bold|Today.>

    <\enumerate>
      <item>From Minkowski to Euclidean: the field equation and
      <strong|Nelson's Euclidean noise>.

      <item>The <strong|field equation at every resolution>; DS equations,
      also pathwise.

      <item>The noise is <strong|sub-Gaussian>, whatever the interaction.

      <item>Infinite volume; appendices: algebra of the flow, counterterms,
      localisation.
    </enumerate>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <cqft-part-title|Observables and field equations>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture<nbsp>5>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Observables of the interacting theory>

    \;

    For <math|G> a functional of the terminal field,
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t><around|[|G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>=G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>,
    <math|G<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>><around|(|G|)>>,
    and

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|t>*G<rsub|t>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>*G<rsub|t>|)>-<around|\<langle\>|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>,\<mathD\>G<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>=0,<space|2em>G<rsub|T>=G:
    </equation*>

    the backward Kolmogorov equation, a Doob transform of the Gaussian heat
    equation. Any solution gives an observable, closed at finite <math|T>,
    not necessarily as <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>. The force <math|F<rsub|t>> is the
    observable of <math|G=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T>>.

    <with|font-series|bold|Tangent observables.>
    <math|V<rsub|T><rsup|\<theta\>>> depending on a coupling,
    <math|U<rsub|t>=\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*V<rsub|t><rsup|\<theta\>>>:

    <\equation*>
      U<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|t>*<around*|[|\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*V<rsub|T><rsup|\<theta\>><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\><rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|T>><around|[|G|]>=-<math-up|Cov><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|T><rsup|\<theta\>>><around*|(|G,\<partial\><rsub|\<theta\>>*V<rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|T>|)>.
    </equation*>

    E.g. the running <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|4>>: for
    <math|\<theta\>=\<lambda\>>: observables <strong|conjugate to the
    coordinates of theory space>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Field equations: Minkowski and Euclidean>

    <\with|font-size|0.9>
      Minkowski (Lecture<nbsp>1): <math|<around|(|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>|)>*\<varphi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=0>
      as an <strong|operator> equation, with the CCR. On time-ordered
      functions <math|\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|2>> also hits the step
      functions:

      <\equation*>
        <around|(|\<Box\><rsub|x>+m<rsup|2>|)>*\<tau\><around|(|x,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>|)>=-\<tau\><around|(|V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)><around|(|x|)>,x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>|)>-\<mathi\>*<big|sum><rsub|j>\<delta\><around|(|x-x<rsub|j>|)>*\<tau\><around|(|\<ldots\>,<wide|x|^><rsub|j>,\<ldots\>|)>.
      </equation*>

      Contact terms: the canonical commutator, a derivative,
      <math|\<mathi\><around|[|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<varphi\><around|(|<math-bf|x>|)>,\<cdummy\>|]>=\<delta\>/\<delta\>\<varphi\><around|(|<math-bf|x>|)>>.
      Rotate, <math|t=-\<mathi\>*\<tau\>>: <math|\<Box\>+m<rsup|2>\<to\>A>,
      <math|-\<mathi\>*\<delta\><around|(|t|)>\<to\>\<delta\><around|(|\<tau\>|)>>,
      and we get the DS equation of Lecture<nbsp>2:

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\>>*<around|[|G<space|0.17em><around|\<langle\>|h,\<omega\>|\<rangle\>>|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\>>*<around|[|\<mathD\><rsub|h>*G|]>,<space|2em>\<omega\>\<assign\>A*\<varphi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>,
      </equation*>

      nonzero: the Euclidean field does not solve <math|A*\<varphi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=0>.

      <\myframed>
        <strong|The noise is the transpose of the derivative:>
        <math|\<mathD\><rsub|h><rsup|\<top\>>=-\<mathD\><rsub|h>+<around|\<langle\>|h,\<omega\>|\<rangle\>>>,
        so <math|<around|\<langle\>|h,\<omega\>|\<rangle\>>=\<mathD\><rsub|h><rsup|\<top\>>1>,
        minus the score of <math|\<nu\>>. Not an independent input: a
        functional of the field, fixed by its law. Free field:
        <math|\<omega\>=A<rsup|1/2>*\<xi\>>, one derivative of white noise.
      </myframed>
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Characterising the noise: two attempts>

    <\with|font-size|0.9>
      <strong|Nelson (1975): condition in real space.> On the lattice <math|\<varepsilon\>\<bbb-Z\><rsup|d>>,
      <math|\<omega\>\<assign\>A*\<varphi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>.
      By the Markov property <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|d>*\<omega\><around|(|x|)>>
      is minus the score of the law of <math|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>> given
      its neighbours:

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\><around|[|\<omega\><around|(|x|)>\|\<varphi\><around|(|y|)>,y\<neq\>x|]>=0,<space|2em>\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<omega\><around|(|x|)>*\<omega\><around|(|y|)>|]>=<around|(|-\<Delta\>+m<rsup|2>+\<bbb-E\>*V<rprime|''><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|)>*\<delta\><around|(|x-y|)>,
      </equation*>

      not Gaussian. A random variable iff
      <math|\<bbb-E\>*V<rprime|''><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>
      in the limit (heuristically): unlikely in <math|d=3> (cf.
      Albeverio\ULiang\UZegarlinski 2006). <math|\<omega\>>: <strong|Nelson's Euclidean noise> (we keep his letter); as far as we know, not studied since
      as a noise.

      <strong|Albeverio\UYoshida (2002): prescribe a Gaussian noise.> Solve
      <math|A*\<psi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=A<rsup|1/2>*\<xi\>>:
      <math|\<psi\>+A<rsup|-1>*V<rprime|'><around|(|\<psi\>|)>> is a free
      field, and formally

      <\equation*>
        <math-up|Law><around|(|\<psi\>|)>\<propto\><around|\||det<around|(|1+A<rsup|-1>*V<rprime|''>|)>|\|>*\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,V<rprime|'>|\<rangle\>>-<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|V<rprime|'>,A<rsup|-1>*V<rprime|'>|\<rangle\>>>*\<mu\><rsup|A<rsup|-1>>\<neq\>\<mathe\><rsup|-V>*\<mu\><rsup|A<rsup|-1>>:
      </equation*>

      nonlocal, not Markov, <strong|no reflection positivity> (only on
      functionals of <math|\<psi\>+A<rsup|-1>*V<rprime|'><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>, a free field).

      <\myframed>
        <strong|Here: condition on the scales.> The noise at resolution
        <math|t> is the conditional expectation of <math|\<omega\>> given the
        coarse field: not Gaussian, but a genuine random variable at each
        resolution, with Gaussian tails.
      </myframed>
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Dyson\USchwinger equations at every resolution>

    \;

    All operators are functions of <math|A> (so they commute);
    <math|K=Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>>, <math|J<rsub|t>=K*Q<rsub|t>> (heat profile:
    <math|J<rsub|t>=\<mathe\><rsup|-A*<around|(|t<rsup|-2>-T<rsup|-2>|)>>\<to\>\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>>
    as <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>).

    The noise observable is the <strong|filtered bridge noise>: with
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>=J<rsub|t>\<Phi\><rsub|T>+\<zeta\><rsub|t>>,
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>=K\<Phi\><rsub|t>-J<rsub|t>F<rsub|t>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>\<zeta\><rsub|t>>.

    <\theorem*>
      <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> The <strong|noise observable>
      <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>=K*\<Phi\><rsub|t>-J<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>> is a
      centred martingale, <math|\<mathd\>\<omega\><rsub|t>=K*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t>-J<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>>,
      and

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>>*<around*|[|\<mathD\><rsub|J<rsub|t>*h>*G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><around*|[|G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)><space|0.17em><around|\<langle\>|h,K*\<Phi\><rsub|t>-J<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>|]>.
      </equation*>

      At <math|t=T>: the DS equation <math|\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|T>>*<around|[|\<mathD\><rsub|h>*G|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|T>><around|[|G<around|\<langle\>|h,K*\<varphi\>+\<mathD\>V<rsub|T>|\<rangle\>>|]>>.
    </theorem*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Proof.> <math|<wide|J|\<dot\>><rsub|t>=K*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>:
    drifts cancel. IBP for <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>> in direction
    <math|J<rsub|t>*h>, using <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>\<sim\>\<nu\><rsub|t>>.

    At <math|t=T>: <math|\<omega\><rsub|T>=K*\<Phi\><rsub|T>+\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>>,
    the Euclidean noise, and <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|\<omega\><rsub|T>|]>>:
    <strong|what an observer at resolution <math|t> knows of it>.
  </hidden>|<\shown>
    <mytitle|Dyson\USchwinger, pathwise>

    \;

    <math|G> a terminal functional, <math|G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>>
    its observable. Then

    <\equation*>
      N<rsub|t>\<assign\>N<rsub|t><around*|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=<around*|(|\<mathD\><rsub|J<rsub|t>*h>*G<rsub|t>|)><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>-G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)><space|0.17em><around|\<langle\>|h,\<omega\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>><space|1em><text|is
      a martingale, >N<rsub|0>=0.
    </equation*>

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Itô>: the drift of <math|\<mathD\><rsub|J<rsub|t>h>G<rsub|t>>
      is <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>G<rsub|t>,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><around|(|K-\<mathD\>F<rsub|t>J<rsub|t>|)>h|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>t>,
      exactly the covariation of the two martingales
      <math|G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>> and
      <math|<around|\<langle\>|h,\<omega\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>>
      (<math|\<mathD\>F<rsub|t>> symmetric). <math|N<rsub|0>=0> since
      <math|J<rsub|0>=0>, <math|\<omega\><rsub|0>=0>.

      <item><math|N> is the <strong|observable of the DS defect>
      <math|\<mathD\><rsub|h>G-G<around|\<langle\>|h,\<omega\><rsub|T>|\<rangle\>>>;
      <math|\<bbb-E\>*N<rsub|t>=0> is DS at resolution <math|t>, and at
      <math|T> the DS equation of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>.

      <item><math|N<rsub|t>> uses only resolution-<math|t> objects:
      <math|G<rsub|t>>, its derivative along <math|J<rsub|t>h>,
      <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>>. No terminal value needed.
    </itemize>

    <\myframed>
      For <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>, <math|\<omega\><rsub|T>> is not
      expected to have a limit, but DS survives as an <strong|identity between observables>:
      <math|N> is a martingale null at <math|0>, for every <math|G>,
      <math|h>.
    </myframed>
  </shown>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The field equation at resolution <math|t>>

    \;

    As <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|K\<to\>A>, and
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>\<assign\>K*\<Phi\><rsub|t>-J<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>>
    becomes

    <\equation*>
      A*\<Phi\><rsub|t>=A*Q<rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>F<rsub|t>+\<omega\><rsub|t>*<space|2em><around|(|A*Q<rsub|t>=\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>><text|
      for the heat profile>|)>:
    </equation*>

    the Euclidean field equation <math|A*\<varphi\>=-V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>+>noise,
    <strong|seen at resolution <math|t>>. No products of distributions: the
    nonlinearity enters only through the force <strong|observable>, smeared
    at scale <math|1/t>. For <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>, with
    <math|F<rsub|t>> in the role of the renormalised cube,
    <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> corresponds to the renormalised DS equation
    (Gubinelli\UHofmanová 2021); for the limit see Lecture<nbsp>7.

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Compare: stochastic quantisation (Lecture<nbsp>3)>

      There the field equation is imposed in an <strong|auxiliary time>; here
      it holds at <strong|each resolution>, and the auxiliary parameter is
      the resolution itself.
    </myframed>

    Conditioning an SPDE solution on a scale filtration gives identities of
    the same shape but with a different noise: the martingale
    <math|J<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>> in
    <math|\<mathd\>\<omega\><rsub|t>> is essential.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The field equation for the forecast>

    <\with|font-size|0.9>
      Substitute <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>=\<psi\><rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>F<rsub|t>>;
      since <math|K*S<rsub|t>=1-J<rsub|t>>, the smoothing disappears:
      <math|K*\<psi\><rsub|t>=F<rsub|t>+\<omega\><rsub|t>>. As
      <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>, with <math|F<rsub|t>=-\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around*|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>:

      <\equation*>
        <wide*|A*\<Phi\><rsub|t>+\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around*|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<omega\><rsub|t>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|<text|Polchinski
        process>><space|3em><wide*|A*\<psi\><rsub|t>+\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<omega\><rsub|t>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|<text|forecast>>
      </equation*>

      <\itemize>
        <item>For the forecast the force acts on the same variable, with no
        smoothing: the Euler\ULagrange equation of
        <math|<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,A*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>+\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>,
        forced by <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>>.

        <item><math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>>, one loop (finite <math|T>, first order in <math|\<lambda\>>):
        <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>\<approx\>V<rsub|T>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|S<rsub|t>\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|T>|)>>
        moves the Wick ordering to <math|c<rsub|t>>:

        <\equation*>
          <around|\<langle\>|\<psi\><rsub|t>,K*\<psi\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>+<frac|\<lambda\>|3>*<big|int><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t><rsup|4>-3*c<rsub|t>*\<psi\><rsub|t><rsup|2>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\><rsub|t>,\<omega\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>+\<ldots\>,
        </equation*>

        and <math|a<rsup|4>-3*c<rsub|t>*a<rsup|2>\<geqslant\><frac|1|2>*a<rsup|4>-<frac|9|2>*c<rsub|t><rsup|2>>
        (since <math|<around|(|a<rsup|2>-3*c<rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0>):
        the <strong|coercivity of the cube> is visible (heuristically; in
        <math|d=3> the mass counterterm still diverges). For
        <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>> it is hidden behind
        <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>>.

        <item>IBP: <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|G<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)><around|\<langle\>|h,K*\<psi\><rsub|t>-F<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>|]>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathD\><rsub|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>\<cal-R\><rsub|t>h>G<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>|]>>.
      </itemize>
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The noise as a posterior mean>

    <\with|font-size|0.85>
      One mode, <math|Q<rsub|t>=t>. Known endpoint <math|y>: a Brownian
      bridge, <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>=<frac|t|T>*y+\<zeta\><rsub|t>> and
      <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>=\<zeta\><rsub|t>/<around|(|T-t|)>\<sim\>N<around|(|0,\<cal-R\><rsub|t>|)>>.
      Random endpoint <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>\<sim\>\<nu\><rsub|T>>:
      <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>-<frac|t|T>*\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>/<around|(|T-t|)>\|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|]>>.

      <\with|par-mode|center>
        <\script-output|tikz|default>
