      </with>

      <small|<with|color|dark grey|<math|T=1>, <math|t=1/2>, <math|\<nu\><rsub|T><around|(|\<mathd\>y|)>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-2*y<rsup|4>+6*y<rsup|2>>N<around|(|0,1|)><around|(|\<mathd\>y|)>> (grey). Green: bridges to the endpoints <math|y<rsub|\<pm\>>>; violet: posterior. The peak at <math|0> in (b): a fold of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>\<mapsto\>\<omega\><rsub|t>> between the wells.>>

      General field: <math|<math-up|Cov><around|(|\<omega\><rsub|t>|)>=\<cal-R\><rsub|t>-S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*J<rsub|t>*\<bbb-E\><around|[|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>|]>*J<rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>=K*J<rsub|t>+J<rsub|t>*\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>|]>*J<rsub|t>>:
      the free bridge noise <strong|minus what the observer cannot resolve>;
      Nelson's mass <math|m<rsup|2>+\<bbb-E\>*V<rprime|''>>, at resolution
      <math|t>, and finite.
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The noise is sub-Gaussian>

    <\with|font-size|0.9>
      <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>> is minus the score of the law of
      <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>> (density <math|p<rsub|t>>), seen through <math|J<rsub|t>>: <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>=-J<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>log
      p<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>. Given <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>>,
      the interacting field is still the Gaussian bridge:

      <\equation*>
        \<Phi\><rsub|t>=J<rsub|t>*\<Phi\><rsub|T>+\<zeta\><rsub|t>,<space|1em>\<zeta\><rsub|t>\<sim\>N<around*|(|0,Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|1-J<rsub|t>|)>|)><space|1em>\<Rightarrow\><space|1em>\<omega\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><around*|[|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<zeta\><rsub|t><mid|\|>\<Phi\><rsub|t>|]>.
      </equation*>

      <\proposition*>
        <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>> is dominated in convex order by
        <math|N<around|(|0,\<cal-R\><rsub|t>|)>>, with
        <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>-Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>> the
        <strong|Wetterich regulator>; in particular

        <\equation*>
          \<bbb-E\>*\<mathe\><rsup|u<around|\<langle\>|h,\<omega\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>>\<leqslant\>exp
          <around*|(|<frac|u<rsup|2>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|h,\<cal-R\><rsub|t>*h|\<rangle\>>|)>,
        </equation*>

        uniformly in the interaction, with no convexity (conditional Jensen).
      </proposition*>

      <\small>
        <\itemize>
          <item>Heat profile, finite spatial cutoff: <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>=A/<around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|A/t<rsup|2>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2>>
          if <math|T=\<infty\>>; <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>\<leqslant\><frac|4|3>*t<rsup|2>> if <math|T\<geqslant\>2*t>.

          <item>With <math|\<mathd\>F<rsub|s>=\<Upsilon\><rsub|s>*\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>> (Lecture<nbsp>4) and Itô:
          <math|\<bbb-E\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Upsilon\><rsub|s><rsup|\<ast\>>*J<rsub|s>*h|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>*\<mathd\>s\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+\<mathe\><rsup|-1>|)>*t<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>:
          a smoothed derivative of the force; no terminal force needed.

          <item><math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>: the noise in
          <math|A*\<Phi\><rsub|t>=A*Q<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>+\<omega\><rsub|t>> is
          controlled a priori; what remains is coercivity of the renormalised
          force.
        </itemize>
      </small>
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|How rough is the noise?>

    Spectral densities, heat profile, <math|T=\<infty\>> (<math|X<rsub|t>>: the free field, <math|V=0>),
    <math|A=<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>>:

    <\equation*>
      <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-lsep|0.8em>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-rsep|0.8em>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|white
      noise>>|<cell|<text|GFF>>|<cell|<text|free noise
      >A*X<rsub|t>>|<cell|<text|bound >\<cal-R\><rsub|t>>>|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|A<rsup|-1>>|<cell|A*\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>>|<cell|<frac|A|\<mathe\><rsup|A/t<rsup|2>>-1>>>>>>
    </equation*>

    <\small>
      <\itemize>
        <item><math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|2>*\<mathe\><rsup|-A/2*t<rsup|2>>>:
        at resolution <math|t>, <math|\<omega\><rsub|t>\<lesssim\>t\<times\>>
        white noise smoothed at scale <math|1/t>, so
        <math|\<omega\><rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=O<around|(|t<rsup|1+d/2>|)>>.

        <item>At scale <math|1/t> this is the size of regularity
        <math|-1-d/2> (<math|-5/2> in <math|d=3>): one derivative rougher
        than white noise, like the free noise <math|A<rsup|1/2>*\<xi\>>,
        <strong|whatever the interaction>.

        <item>Not a limit distribution: on a fixed <math|h> only
        <math|<around|\<langle\>|h,\<omega\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>=O<around|(|t|)>>, while the free noise stays bounded.

        <item>On resolved modes (<math|<around|\||k|\|>\<ll\>t>) the bound exceeds the free noise
        by <math|t<rsup|2>/A>; it is attained by a pinning potential
        <math|\<kappa\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>, <math|\<kappa\>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

        <item><strong|Pathwise> (convex domination of the norm, Doob): a.s.
        for <math|t\<geqslant\>1> (<math|t\<leqslant\>T/2> if <math|T\<less\>\<infty\>>), at finite spatial cutoff, with a random constant <math|<wide|C|~>>,

        <\equation*>
          sup<rsub|x><around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)><rsup|-\<delta\>><around|\||\<omega\><rsub|t><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|C|~>*t<rsup|1+d/2><sqrt|log
          <around|(|2+t|)>>.
        </equation*>
      </itemize>
    </small>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The noise as a stochastic observable>

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<leqslant\>T>> is a
      <strong|martingale>, with terminal value Nelson's Euclidean noise
      <math|K*\<Phi\><rsub|T>+\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>>.

      <item>At each resolution a <strong|smooth random field>; every
      <math|<around|\<langle\>|h,\<omega\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>> is
      <strong|sub-Gaussian> with variance
      <math|\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|h,\<cal-R\><rsub|t>*h|\<rangle\>>>,
      uniformly in the interaction and the cutoff.

      <item>Paired with functionals of the coarse field it is the
      <strong|transpose of the smoothed derivative>
      <math|\<mathD\><rsub|J<rsub|t>h>> (DS at every scale).

      <item>Expected <strong|local> in the sense of Lecture<nbsp>3: germs
      <math|A*\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|x|)>+n<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|x|)>|)>>,
      <math|n<rsub|t>> a renormalised cube; local iff the force is. For
      <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>: local but not closed (no terminal representative).

      

      <item>For <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>,
      <math|A*\<varphi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>> is not expected
      to be a random variable (Nelson; Albeverio\ULiang\UZegarlinski), but
      its conditional expectations are, consistently:
      <math|\<omega\><rsub|s>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*\<omega\><rsub|t>>.
    </itemize>

    <\myframed>
      The Euclidean field equation of <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> has a
      well-defined sub-Gaussian noise: an <strong|observable without a
      terminal representative>.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Infinite volume>

    \;

    On <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>>, the free energy <math|V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>> and
    <math|V<rsub|t>> are <strong|extensive> (<math|\<sim\>L<rsup|d>>). Three
    <strong|intensive> objects survive:

    <\enumerate-roman>
      <item><with|font-series|bold|the force.> For
      <math|V<rsub|T>=<big|int>v<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>, on
      <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>:

      <\equation*>
        \<Phi\><rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*F<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=-\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|v<rsub|T><rprime|'><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T><around|(|x|)>|)>|]>.
      </equation*>

      Everything is local; infinite-volume limits should solve this.
      Uniqueness of solutions <math|\<leftrightarrow\>> uniqueness of the
      theory; non-uniqueness expected at phase transitions
      (<math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>, deep double well: Glimm\UJaffe\USpencer 1975).

      <item><with|font-series|bold|the Burgers flow> of the force (Lecture<nbsp>4);

      <item><with|font-series|bold|differences of free energies>
      <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|T><rsup|<around|(|L|)>><around|(|f|)>=-log<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-f>\<mathd\>\<nu\><rsub|T><rsup|<around|(|L|)>>>, <math|f>
      local: bounded as <math|L\<to\>\<infty\>>; basis of the infinite-volume
      analysis of <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>> and <math|exp
      <around|(|\<Phi\>|)><rsub|2>>.
    </enumerate-roman>

    Estimates must be <strong|local> (weighted norms, volume-independent
    constants): the analogue of \Pcoming down from infinity\Q for the parabolic dynamics.
    <small|<with|color|dark grey|Barashkov\UGubinelli, PMP 2023>>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Differences of free energies>

    <\with|font-size|0.9>

      Perturb the optimal drift <math|u<rsup|\<ast\>>> of the Boué\UDupuis formula (Lecture<nbsp>4), <math|X<rsup|u<rsup|\<ast\>>>=\<Phi\>>: <math|u=u<rsup|\<ast\>>+w>,
      <math|X<rsup|u>=\<Phi\>+I<around|(|w|)>>,
      <math|I<rsub|t><around|(|w|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*w<rsub|s>\<mathd\>s>.
      Euler\ULagrange gives the cross term

      <\equation*>
        \<bbb-E\><big|int><around|\<langle\>|u<rsup|\<ast\>>,w|\<rangle\>>=-\<bbb-E\><around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>,I<rsub|T><around|(|w|)>|\<rangle\>>.
      </equation*>

      <\proposition*>
        <dueto|Relative variational principle><with|font-shape|right|(formal)> For <math|f> bounded below,

        <\equation*>
          -log <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-f>*\<mathd\>\<nu\><rsub|T>=inf<rsub|w>
          \<bbb-E\><around*|[|f<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>+I<rsub|T><around|(|w|)>|)>+B<rsub|V<rsub|T>><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>,I<rsub|T><around|(|w|)>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|]>,
        </equation*>

        over adapted <math|w> with <math|\<bbb-E\><big|int><around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>s\<less\>\<infty\>>: the two extensive infima cancel exactly.
      </proposition*>

      <math|B<rsub|V><around|(|\<phi\>,h|)>=V<around|(|\<phi\>+h|)>-V<around|(|\<phi\>|)>-<around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>V<around|(|\<phi\>|)>,h|\<rangle\>>>:
      the <strong|first-order Taylor remainder>.

      <\itemize>
        <item>Minimiser: <math|\<Phi\><rsup|f>=\<Phi\>+I<around|(|w<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>> solves
        the FBSDE with terminal potential <math|V<rsub|T>+f>, a perturbation of
        <math|\<Phi\>>:

        <\equation*>
          F<rsup|f><rsub|s>-F<rsub|s>=-\<bbb-E\><rsub|s><around|[|\<mathD\>f<around|(|\<Phi\><rsup|f><rsub|T>|)>+\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsup|f><rsub|T>|)>-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>.
        </equation*>
      </itemize>
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Infinite-volume measures>

    Local interaction <math|V<rsub|T>=<big|int>v<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>|)>*\<mathd\>x>:

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|V<rsub|T>><around|(|\<phi\>,h|)>=<big|int>b<rsub|T><around|(|\<phi\><around|(|x|)>,h<around|(|x|)>|)>*\<mathd\>x,<space|2em>b<rsub|T><around|(|a,h|)>=v<rsub|T><around|(|a+h|)>-v<rsub|T><around|(|a|)>-v<rsub|T><rprime|'><around|(|a|)>*h.
    </equation*>

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|b<rsub|T><around|(|a,0|)>=0>: only the region where the
      perturbation acts counts. Every term is <strong|intensive>: no free
      energy, only <math|\<Phi\>> and its perturbation.

      <item><math|f> local <math|\<Rightarrow\>> the optimal <math|w> should
      decay away from its support, at the rate of decay of correlations.

      <item>Several solutions of the FBSDE (phase transitions)
      <math|\<Rightarrow\>> several measures, each with its own relative variational principle.

      <item><math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>: the remainder term carries the
      counterterms; the change of variables of Barashkov\UGubinelli (Duke 2020) is still
      needed.
    </itemize>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Formal statement> (at fixed UV cutoff <math|T>). An infinite-volume measure is the law of
      <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> for a solution of the infinite-volume FBSDE
      satisfying the relative variational principle for all local <math|f>.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
